{"sections":[],"topicSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Type Methods","identifiers":["doc:\/\/SwiduxPersistence\/documentation\/SwiduxPersistence\/EntityCollapse\/byID(preferring:)"],"anchor":"Type-Methods"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/swiduxpersistence\/entitycollapse"]}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"EntityCollapse"}],"symbolKind":"enum","modules":[{"name":"SwiduxPersistence"}],"fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"EntityCollapse","kind":"identifier"}],"externalID":"s:17SwiduxPersistence14EntityCollapseO","roleHeading":"Enumeration","title":"EntityCollapse"},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/SwiduxPersistence\/documentation\/SwiduxPersistence\/EntityCollapse","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiduxPersistence\/documentation\/SwiduxPersistence"]]},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Ready-made collapse resolvers."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"EntityCollapse"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"Why-the-hook-takes-the-whole-collection","text":"Why the hook takes the whole collection","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A per-ID resolver would only handle half the problem. Rows sharing an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"id"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"are one failure mode; the other is a "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"singleton","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — an entity an app holds"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"exactly one of, such as a settings record. Created independently on two"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"fresh installs, two singletons have "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"different","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" UUIDs, so nothing groups"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"them and no ID-keyed resolver can ever see the conflict. A"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"([E]) -> [E]","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" hook covers both, plus general garbage collection, with one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"API."}]},{"text":"The determinism requirement","level":2,"anchor":"The-determinism-requirement","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A resolver must be a pure function of "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"replicated content"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" — never"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"persistentModelID","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", never a local timestamp, never array position. Every"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"device must compute the same survivor set from the same rows. A resolver"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that doesn’t reintroduces the mutual-tombstone loss the write path is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"designed to avoid: two devices pick different survivors, each tombstones the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"other’s, and after sync "},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"both","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"type":"text","text":" rows are gone."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"It must also be idempotent — ","type":"text"},{"code":"collapse(collapse(rows))","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" must keep the same","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"IDs as ","type":"text"},{"code":"collapse(rows)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". This is checked with an ","type":"text"},{"code":"assert","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" in debug builds."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Collapse removes IDs, not rows","level":2,"anchor":"Collapse-removes-IDs-not-rows","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A resolver’s output is read as "},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"the set of IDs that should exist and what","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"each should contain","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"type":"text","text":". An ID the resolver drops is deleted, including every"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"row carrying it. Several rows that share a "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"surviving"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" ID are all updated"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"to the survivor’s value, and none of them is deleted."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"That asymmetry is deliberate, and it is the whole reason the singleton case"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is the one this hook really solves. Deleting an ID is safe because "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"id"},{"type":"text","text":" is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"replicated: every device independently computes the same set of doomed IDs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and issues the same deletions. Deleting one of several rows that share an ID"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"not"}]},{"type":"text","text":" safe — nothing replicated distinguishes them, so two devices can"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pick different physical rows to keep, each tombstones the other’s, and both"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"are lost."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Rows sharing an ID need no deletion anyway: writes already converge across","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"every matching row and reads already collapse to one value per ID (see"},{"type":"text","t